73
00:04:28,220 --> 00:04:34,500
From anchorage to anchorage, the structure extends 6,690 feet.

74
00:04:34,600 --> 00:04:40,480
The fantastic Verrazano flaunts a 4,260-foot center span

75
00:04:40,580 --> 00:04:43,640
and double-decked six-lane roadway.

76
00:04:43,641 --> 00:04:46,940
Amon said the bridge was conceived as an enormous object

77
00:04:47,040 --> 00:04:49,580
drawn as faintly as possible.

78
00:04:49,680 --> 00:04:52,880
Ed Fennell's father worked on the cables.

79
00:04:52,980 --> 00:04:56,280
The younger, more crazy iron workers

80
00:04:56,380 --> 00:04:57,880
would take their American Bridge hard hat.

81
00:04:57,980 --> 00:04:59,180
It was like a bowl.

82
00:04:59,280 --> 00:05:04,300
And the cable was getting weaved like this, the main cable.

83
00:05:04,400 --> 00:05:07,640
And they would sit in their hard hat and slide down the cable

84
00:05:07,740 --> 00:05:09,500
to get the lunch.

85
00:05:09,600 --> 00:05:11,400
It took six months for the crew

86
00:05:11,401 --> 00:05:14,900
to spin the four main cables that support the roadway,

87
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:19,300
testing each wire for size, strength, and metallic content.

88
00:05:19,400 --> 00:05:22,040
Then the cables were carried from anchorage to anchorage

89
00:05:22,140 --> 00:05:24,980
on a wheel that laid them side by side and one

90
00:05:25,080 --> 00:05:26,140
above the other.

91
00:05:26,240 --> 00:05:27,720
It's called the eternal wheel.

92
00:05:27,820 --> 00:05:31,820
And it was hung on a cable over where the main cable would lay.

93
00:05:31,920 --> 00:05:35,520
And it just ran 24 hours a day in a Verrazano bridge.

94
00:05:35,620 --> 00:05:38,300
In a suspension design, the parallel cables

95
00:05:38,400 --> 00:05:40,900
are the main support member.

96
00:05:40,901 --> 00:05:42,760
The cables are strung over towers

97
00:05:42,860 --> 00:05:44,940
that run the length of the bridge

98
00:05:45,040 --> 00:05:47,940
and are anchored at each end.

99
00:05:48,040 --> 00:05:51,280
These are huge blocks, heavy blocks.

100
00:05:51,380 --> 00:05:56,220
And their purpose is to hold the cable, to resist the cable.

101
00:05:56,320 --> 00:05:58,480
And then you have the cable goes to the other end,

102
00:05:58,580 --> 00:06:00,020
to the other anchorage.

103
00:06:00,120 --> 00:06:02,080
And it's anchored in the anchorage.

104
00:06:02,180 --> 00:06:06,800
Typically, the cables would splay out into bands of wires,

105
00:06:06,900 --> 00:06:10,900
groups of wires that would wrap around eye bars, steel beams,

106
00:06:10,901 --> 00:06:14,100
that are then embedded in the cable, in the anchorage.

107
00:06:14,200 --> 00:06:16,200
And they're embedded further and further.

108
00:06:16,300 --> 00:06:19,000
And then you get to the very base of the anchorage.

109
00:06:19,100 --> 00:06:20,100
And there's a plate.

110
00:06:20,200 --> 00:06:21,940
It's attached to a plate.

111
00:06:22,040 --> 00:06:28,120
Now, on top of that plate is masonry and a very heavy block

112
00:06:28,220 --> 00:06:29,480
that resists that pull.

113
00:06:29,580 --> 00:06:33,020
So you've got the cable wanting to be pulled this way.

114
00:06:33,120 --> 00:06:34,760
And it's trying to lift this block up.

115
00:06:34,860 --> 00:06:37,520
And that's what holds it.

116
00:06:37,620 --> 00:06:40,000
Suspenders hung from the main cables

117
00:06:40,001 --> 00:06:41,460
support the deck.

118
00:06:41,560 --> 00:06:44,000
It was the longest suspension bridge in the world

119
00:06:44,100 --> 00:06:48,700
and held that record until 1981.

120
00:06:48,800 --> 00:06:51,540
Named for the 16th century Italian explorer who

121
00:06:51,640 --> 00:06:54,540
discovered New York Harbor, the Verrazano Narrows

122
00:06:54,640 --> 00:06:59,760
was dedicated on November 21, 1964.

123
00:06:59,860 --> 00:07:02,360
When it came time to present Othmar Amun,

124
00:07:02,460 --> 00:07:04,900
Robert Moses introduced the 85-year-old

125
00:07:05,000 --> 00:07:07,360
as the greatest living bridge engineer,

126
00:07:07,361 --> 00:07:09,760
perhaps the greatest of all time.

127
00:07:09,860 --> 00:07:11,960
Although Amon is credited with many bridges,

128
00:07:12,060 --> 00:07:15,060
the Verrazano Narrows was his consummate achievement.

129
00:07:18,340 --> 00:07:21,800
Coming up, a medieval symbol of New York City.

130
00:07:24,940 --> 00:07:28,820
The Verrazano Narrows broke San Francisco's Golden Gate Bridge

131
00:07:28,920 --> 00:07:32,020
span record by 60 feet.

132
00:07:32,120 --> 00:07:35,220
New York bridges will return on Modern Marvels.

133
00:07:37,360 --> 00:07:42,360
We now return to New York Bridges on Modern Marvels.

134
00:07:42,460 --> 00:07:46,500
East of Liberty Island, joining the island of Manhattan

135
00:07:46,600 --> 00:07:50,600
to Brooklyn, is the Brooklyn Bridge.

136
00:07:50,700 --> 00:07:55,620
After 13 years of construction, in the spring of 1883,

137
00:07:55,720 --> 00:07:58,980
New Yorkers marveled at their new bridge,

138
00:07:59,080 --> 00:08:03,580
framed against a sky brilliant with fireworks.

139
00:08:07,360 --> 00:08:10,860
Spanning the East River with its neo-gothic towers

140
00:08:10,960 --> 00:08:14,000
and dense network of cables, the Brooklyn Bridge

141
00:08:14,100 --> 00:08:16,940
was the largest suspension bridge in the world.

142
00:08:17,040 --> 00:08:20,400
So impressive, New Yorkers compared it to the pyramids,

143
00:08:20,500 --> 00:08:23,340
and it hadn't opened for carriage traffic a minute too

144
00:08:23,440 --> 00:08:24,740
soon.

145
00:08:24,840 --> 00:08:27,420
Manhattan had been practically nothing in 1800.

146
00:08:27,520 --> 00:08:31,380
By 1875, there were a million people on Manhattan Island.

147
00:08:31,480 --> 00:08:34,420
By 1910, there were going to be 2 million people.

148
00:08:34,421 --> 00:08:36,560
there were going to be 2 million people,

149
00:08:36,660 --> 00:08:38,260
and they could see it coming.

150
00:08:38,360 --> 00:08:40,160
And they were desperate for space.

151
00:08:40,260 --> 00:08:42,900
An East River Bridge could solve overcrowding

152
00:08:43,000 --> 00:08:46,040
on the island's Lower East Side and spur development

153
00:08:46,140 --> 00:08:47,600
of Brooklyn as well.

154
00:08:47,700 --> 00:08:50,600
A joint venture, it was originally called the New York

155
00:08:50,700 --> 00:08:52,100
and Brooklyn Bridge.

156
00:08:52,200 --> 00:08:54,580
After the city's consolidation in 1898,

157
00:08:54,680 --> 00:08:57,140
the New York name was dropped.

158
00:08:57,240 --> 00:09:00,520
The Brooklyn Bridge cost an enormous amount of money

159
00:09:00,620 --> 00:09:02,940
to build at the time that it was constructed.

160
00:09:02,941 --> 00:09:07,540
It required the resources of a much larger city than just

161
00:09:07,640 --> 00:09:10,180
New York, the island of Manhattan at the time,

162
00:09:10,280 --> 00:09:12,680
or the city of Brooklyn at the time.

163
00:09:12,780 --> 00:09:15,180
And the two of them had to come together.

164
00:09:15,280 --> 00:09:17,560
Before the Brooklyn Bridge, the only way

165
00:09:17,660 --> 00:09:21,520
to cross the unpredictable East River was by ferry.

166
00:09:21,620 --> 00:09:23,760
And we may think they're romantic today because we

167
00:09:23,860 --> 00:09:25,300
don't have to depend on them.

168
00:09:25,400 --> 00:09:27,660
But believe me, when that was the only way you could cross

169
00:09:27,760 --> 00:09:29,600
the river, you hated them.

170
00:09:29,700 --> 00:09:32,880
In 1865, over 41 people were

171
00:09:32,940 --> 00:09:37,180
people crossed the East River by ferry, sandwiched on boats

172
00:09:37,280 --> 00:09:39,580
moving between Brooklyn and Manhattan.

173
00:09:39,680 --> 00:09:42,280
East River passengers were as likely to lose their lives

174
00:09:42,380 --> 00:09:44,580
as make their destination.

175
00:09:44,680 --> 00:09:47,080
One miserable commuter was legendary engineer

176
00:09:47,180 --> 00:09:49,180
John Augustus Roebling.

177
00:09:49,280 --> 00:09:52,400
Roebling pushed for a suspension bridge over the East River.

178
00:09:52,500 --> 00:09:55,800
As a famous bridge designer and owner of a wire rope company,

179
00:09:55,900 --> 00:09:58,800
Roebling wanted to build the bridge himself.

180
00:09:58,900 --> 00:10:01,400
His proposal was met with mixed reaction

181
00:10:01,401 --> 00:10:04,640
due to the fact that many of the turn of the century bridges

182
00:10:04,740 --> 00:10:06,640
collapsed.

183
00:10:06,740 --> 00:10:10,640
Along comes Roebling with a design for an almost 1600-foot

184
00:10:10,740 --> 00:10:13,340
span, unthought of, unheard of.

185
00:10:13,440 --> 00:10:16,440
This is the moonshot of the mid 19th century.

186
00:10:16,540 --> 00:10:20,260
What basically made the Brooklyn Bridge possible

187
00:10:20,360 --> 00:10:23,560
were improvements in wire technology,

188
00:10:23,660 --> 00:10:27,160
much in the same way that improvements in elevator

189
00:10:27,260 --> 00:10:31,400
technology made it possible to build the skyscraper.

190
00:10:31,401 --> 00:10:34,600
They were able to come up with a wire that was strong enough

191
00:10:34,700 --> 00:10:39,040
to bridge the East River without snapping.

192
00:10:39,140 --> 00:10:41,680
John Augustus Roebling's wire rope cable

193
00:10:41,780 --> 00:10:46,020
was the breakthrough in modern suspension bridge technology.

194
00:10:46,120 --> 00:10:50,220
In 1869, the same year the bridge's plan was approved,

195
00:10:50,320 --> 00:10:53,120
John Roebling died of tetmiss.

196
00:10:53,220 --> 00:10:56,900
He would never see as cobweb of cables reached to the sky.

197
00:10:57,000 --> 00:10:59,100
The entire construction of the Brooklyn Bridge

198
00:10:59,101 --> 00:11:03,040
fell to his 32-year-old son, Washington.

199
00:11:03,140 --> 00:11:05,140
One year later, Washington Roebling

200
00:11:05,240 --> 00:11:08,380
ordered pneumatic caissons floated onto the East River

201
00:11:08,480 --> 00:11:10,540
and sunk into the riverbed.

202
00:11:10,640 --> 00:11:13,340
Inside these wooden subterranean chambers,

203
00:11:13,440 --> 00:11:18,780
immigrant labor paid $2.25 a day mined for bedrock.

204
00:11:18,880 --> 00:11:21,820
They needed a firm foundation for the bridges' towers,

205
00:11:21,920 --> 00:11:24,600
which would stand in the river.

206
00:11:24,700 --> 00:11:27,820
They would begin to put successive amounts

207
00:11:27,821 --> 00:11:31,120
of stone on top of the caissons.

208
00:11:31,220 --> 00:11:35,020
And this weight would push the caisson down.

209
00:11:35,120 --> 00:11:37,460
Compressed air was pumped into the caissons

210
00:11:37,560 --> 00:11:39,740
to keep them from sinking into the mud.

211
00:11:39,840 --> 00:11:43,640
As the depth increased, so did the air pressure.

212
00:11:43,740 --> 00:11:47,100
And then they would send the workers in there with shovels

213
00:11:47,200 --> 00:11:48,700
and picks and so forth.

214
00:11:48,800 --> 00:11:52,580
And they would literally start excavating all of the material

215
00:11:52,680 --> 00:11:54,440
that's in the caisson.

216
00:11:54,540 --> 00:11:56,880
Workers entering and leaving through an airlock

217
00:11:56,881 --> 00:11:59,780
experienced excruciating pain.

218
00:11:59,880 --> 00:12:03,420
The changes in air pressure of the caissons proved deadly.

219
00:12:03,520 --> 00:12:06,420
Caissons disease, also known as the bends,

220
00:12:06,520 --> 00:12:09,000
killed three men and paralyzed others,

221
00:12:09,100 --> 00:12:11,320
including Washington Roebling.

222
00:12:11,420 --> 00:12:14,540
They thought it had something to do with poor oxygen

223
00:12:14,640 --> 00:12:16,540
quality in the caissons.

224
00:12:16,640 --> 00:12:21,680
They thought that the men were in too cramped conditions.

225
00:12:21,780 --> 00:12:25,000
They even looked at the mud to make sure that there wasn't

226
00:12:25,001 --> 00:12:28,400
that there wasn't some kind of gaseous exhalation

227
00:12:28,500 --> 00:12:30,900
that the mud was giving off when they were doing

228
00:12:31,000 --> 00:12:33,640
the excavation work within the caissons.

229
00:12:33,740 --> 00:12:37,980
Permanently unable to walk or talk from the crippling bends,

230
00:12:38,080 --> 00:12:42,220
Washington Roebling became known as the man in the window.

231
00:12:42,320 --> 00:12:44,620
He supervised the bridge from his townhouse

232
00:12:44,720 --> 00:12:49,620
in Brooklyn Heights using Emily, his wife, as an intermediary.

233
00:12:49,720 --> 00:12:52,200
It was dangerous, but it was work.

234
00:12:52,300 --> 00:12:54,560
And this was important to the immigrants.

235
00:12:54,561 --> 00:12:55,820
Now, they were paid very little.

236
00:12:55,920 --> 00:12:57,400
They went on strike, and actually, I believe

237
00:12:57,500 --> 00:12:59,600
the strike was broken.

238
00:12:59,700 --> 00:13:01,500
Because after all, the bridge builders could always

239
00:13:01,600 --> 00:13:04,300
go out there and scoop up another bunch of immigrants.

240
00:13:04,400 --> 00:13:07,540
Digging through the riverbed of loose mud, silt, and stones,

241
00:13:07,640 --> 00:13:11,780
the Brooklyn Foundation hit bedrock at 44 feet.

242
00:13:11,880 --> 00:13:15,840
The foundation on the Manhattan end rested on sand.

243
00:13:15,940 --> 00:13:18,220
A geologist as well as an engineer,

244
00:13:18,320 --> 00:13:20,580
Washington Roebling learned from his borings

245
00:13:20,581 --> 00:13:24,180
that in Manhattan, bedrock was about 175 feet

246
00:13:24,280 --> 00:13:25,980
below the riverbed.

247
00:13:26,080 --> 00:13:28,520
He'd reached about 78 feet.

248
00:13:28,620 --> 00:13:30,820
Roebling would not risk more men.

249
00:13:30,920 --> 00:13:34,460
So after an analysis of the stratum from his boring,

250
00:13:34,560 --> 00:13:37,060
he determined that if the particular layer that the tower

251
00:13:37,160 --> 00:13:39,640
would be sitting on, which was compressed silt,

252
00:13:39,740 --> 00:13:42,740
had not moved in the past 100 million years,

253
00:13:42,840 --> 00:13:44,940
most likely it would never move.

254
00:13:45,040 --> 00:13:47,000
Roebling put an end to the excavation,

255
00:13:47,100 --> 00:13:49,040
finished construction of the towers,

256
00:13:49,041 --> 00:13:52,280
and began to lay the cables that held up the framework

257
00:13:52,380 --> 00:13:54,340
of the bridge.

258
00:13:54,440 --> 00:14:00,180
A rope, a very thin line, would be shot across the East River

259
00:14:00,280 --> 00:14:02,420
or hauled across the East River.

260
00:14:02,520 --> 00:14:04,360
And attached to this piece of rope

261
00:14:04,460 --> 00:14:08,700
was the first strand of wire, some of which

262
00:14:08,800 --> 00:14:12,860
is actually thinner in diameter than a human hair.

263
00:14:12,960 --> 00:14:16,760
And they would painstakingly pull the first of the wire

264
00:14:16,761 --> 00:14:20,260
across, hang them over the top of the towers,

265
00:14:20,360 --> 00:14:22,900
and affix them to the anchorages that

266
00:14:23,000 --> 00:14:27,300
sit on either end of the bridge and anchor the cables

267
00:14:27,400 --> 00:14:28,900
to the earth.

268
00:14:29,000 --> 00:14:31,200
The cable was made up of a large number

269
00:14:31,300 --> 00:14:35,520
of individual wires which were parallel to one another.

270
00:14:35,620 --> 00:14:39,520
Neither the cables nor the wires were twisted in any way.

271
00:14:39,620 --> 00:14:43,260
Suspended from a board seat with ropes at its corners,

272
00:14:43,261 --> 00:14:47,860
Men traveled between the towers on pulleys.

273
00:14:47,960 --> 00:14:51,540
And then when they had a bundle of a couple of thousand

274
00:14:51,640 --> 00:14:54,740
or a few thousand of those strands,

275
00:14:54,840 --> 00:14:57,200
they would have a machine that came along that

276
00:14:57,300 --> 00:14:59,580
was like a large spinner.

277
00:14:59,680 --> 00:15:03,380
And it would basically pull the strands together,

278
00:15:03,480 --> 00:15:05,020
and it would bind them.

279
00:15:05,120 --> 00:15:07,980
They'd repeat the process until they had over a dozen

280
00:15:08,080 --> 00:15:09,360
bundles of wire.

281
00:15:09,460 --> 00:15:13,260
Then they would be bound into one large cable.

282
00:15:13,261 --> 00:15:16,600
which are the main cables seen on the bridge today.

283
00:15:16,700 --> 00:15:19,840
It was the longest suspension bridge in the world.

284
00:15:19,940 --> 00:15:23,600
The wires on the bridge, steel wires

285
00:15:23,700 --> 00:15:27,180
with a coating of zinc, a galvanized coating,

286
00:15:27,280 --> 00:15:31,940
provided tremendous strength and also provided tremendous

287
00:15:32,040 --> 00:15:34,280
resistance to corrosion.

288
00:15:34,380 --> 00:15:39,920
Each of the four steel cables could hold over 11,000 tons.

289
00:15:40,020 --> 00:15:41,660
It was determined in later years

290
00:15:41,661 --> 00:15:44,160
that the Brooklyn Bridge was six times stronger

291
00:15:44,260 --> 00:15:48,060
than necessary for the time in which it was built.

292
00:15:48,160 --> 00:15:50,840
For everyday New Yorkers, the bridge's strength

293
00:15:50,940 --> 00:15:54,240
was every bit as important as its view.

294
00:15:54,340 --> 00:15:57,040
The first great bridge had a promenade that

295
00:15:57,140 --> 00:15:59,440
was purposely centered in the middle of the bridge

296
00:15:59,540 --> 00:16:03,380
and raised above all the traffic and all of the barriers

297
00:16:03,480 --> 00:16:07,020
so that pedestrians would have the finest view.

298
00:16:07,120 --> 00:16:08,660
And indeed, they did.

299
00:16:08,760 --> 00:16:10,960
John Roebley, the designer of the Brooklyn Bridge,

300
00:16:10,961 --> 00:16:14,300
considered it a boulevard in the sky.

301
00:16:14,400 --> 00:16:18,140
From the bridge, New Yorkers could glimpse their future

302
00:16:18,240 --> 00:16:20,040
as a world-class metropolis.

303
00:16:23,140 --> 00:16:26,240
Just north of the Brooklyn Bridge and the Manhattan Bridge

304
00:16:26,340 --> 00:16:29,940
is the mechanical-looking Williamsburg,

305
00:16:30,040 --> 00:16:32,780
built because for all the hoopla, the Brooklyn Bridge

306
00:16:32,880 --> 00:16:36,980
just hadn't gotten the job done.

307
00:16:37,080 --> 00:16:40,160
Ferries were still packed in the 1890s,

308
00:16:40,161 --> 00:16:43,460
And New Yorkers were frantically lobbying for a second East

309
00:16:43,560 --> 00:16:45,440
River Bridge.

310
00:16:45,540 --> 00:16:48,960
It was logical that Manhattan be bridged to Williamsburg

311
00:16:49,060 --> 00:16:51,780
because Williamsburg was a bustling port

312
00:16:51,880 --> 00:16:54,540
and home to the super rich owners of the companies

313
00:16:54,640 --> 00:16:55,780
based there.

314
00:16:55,880 --> 00:17:00,040
Under pressure, chief engineer Leffert L. Buck and architect

315
00:17:00,140 --> 00:17:04,520
Henry Hornbostell erected a suspension bridge in just seven

316
00:17:04,620 --> 00:17:07,060
years, less than half the time it

317
00:17:07,160 --> 00:17:10,160
took to build the elder statesman Brooklyn Bridge.

318
00:17:10,161 --> 00:17:14,160
The bridge goes back with my own history of my parents,

319
00:17:14,260 --> 00:17:16,600
who were both immigrants from Europe,

320
00:17:16,700 --> 00:17:18,300
who ended up on the Lower East Side.

321
00:17:18,400 --> 00:17:20,000
It's a very typical story.

322
00:17:20,100 --> 00:17:23,300
And they crossed the bridge, the big bridge, to Brooklyn,

323
00:17:23,400 --> 00:17:25,800
and then lived in Williamsburg on the other end.

324
00:17:25,900 --> 00:17:28,740
And my mother even told me stories on very hot nights

325
00:17:28,840 --> 00:17:32,820
that they would go out and they would sleep on the bridge.

326
00:17:32,920 --> 00:17:35,260
The steel-towered Williamsburg Bridge

327
00:17:35,360 --> 00:17:39,920
was begun in 1896 and opened with great fanfare

328
00:17:39,921 --> 00:17:42,920
in 1903.

329
00:17:43,020 --> 00:17:45,920
With a main span of 1,600 feet,

330
00:17:46,020 --> 00:17:48,920
it was the longest suspension bridge in the world,

331
00:17:49,020 --> 00:17:52,460
wrenching from the Brooklyn Bridge a title it held for 20

332
00:17:52,560 --> 00:17:54,160
years.

333
00:17:54,260 --> 00:17:56,880
One of the things that the Williamsburg Bridge did

334
00:17:56,980 --> 00:17:59,380
was give these poor residents of the Lower East Side,

335
00:17:59,480 --> 00:18:01,280
and by the time it opened and really got going,

336
00:18:01,380 --> 00:18:05,880
it was the Jews, it gave them an escape route across the river

337
00:18:05,980 --> 00:18:08,080
to Williamsburg, and they took it.

338
00:18:08,081 --> 00:18:11,380
If you were poor, if you were working on the Lower East Side

339
00:18:11,480 --> 00:18:12,780
and you were living in Williamsburg,

340
00:18:12,880 --> 00:18:15,720
which was a common trip, that's how you got back and forth.

341
00:18:15,820 --> 00:18:18,260
And these bridges enabled you to do that.

342
00:18:18,360 --> 00:18:19,960
They cared for the poor.

343
00:18:20,060 --> 00:18:23,200
Once the bridge was up, previous well-to-do citizens

344
00:18:23,300 --> 00:18:25,260
of Williamsburg fled.

345
00:18:25,360 --> 00:18:27,200
When the penniless foreigners arrived,

346
00:18:27,300 --> 00:18:30,200
six-story tenements waited to house them.

347
00:18:30,300 --> 00:18:33,200
By 1910, Williamsburg had the densest population

348
00:18:33,300 --> 00:18:36,100
and highest infant mortality rate in all

349
00:18:36,101 --> 00:18:39,600
in all of the newly unified greater New York.

350
00:18:39,700 --> 00:18:42,540
The Williamsburg became known as Jews' Bridge

351
00:18:42,640 --> 00:18:44,500
because Jewish immigrants walk daily

352
00:18:44,600 --> 00:18:46,740
between their old neighborhood in Brooklyn

353
00:18:46,840 --> 00:18:48,980
and their new neighborhood in Williamsburg.

354
00:18:49,080 --> 00:18:52,020
And walking was the major mode of transportation.

355
00:18:52,120 --> 00:18:53,280
So they built promenades.

356
00:18:53,380 --> 00:18:55,080
This is 16 feet wide.

357
00:18:55,180 --> 00:18:56,980
They had a second one on the other side of the bridge,

358
00:18:57,080 --> 00:19:01,460
16 feet wide, 32 feet devoted to pedestrians.

359
00:19:01,560 --> 00:19:04,260
And the pedestrians filled every inch of it.

360
00:19:04,261 --> 00:19:06,860
With its cumbersome lattice construction,

361
00:19:06,960 --> 00:19:11,200
the overall effect aesthetically was an ungainly mess.

362
00:19:11,300 --> 00:19:14,260
One unkind critic of the day, John DeWitt Warner,

363
00:19:14,360 --> 00:19:17,240
described it as the surrender of the city beautiful

364
00:19:17,340 --> 00:19:18,840
to the city vulgar.

365
00:19:18,940 --> 00:19:22,820
This bridge is suspended, held up by just four cables.

366
00:19:22,920 --> 00:19:25,420
Each cable is 19 inches in diameter,

367
00:19:25,520 --> 00:19:30,260
has 7,696 parallel pencil thin wires,

368
00:19:30,360 --> 00:19:33,960
and they spread out into 37 strands in the anchorage.

369
00:19:33,961 --> 00:19:39,220
Those strands, each strand is wrapped around an eye bar, which looks like the eye of a needle.

370
00:19:39,800 --> 00:19:46,380
And those eye bars are embedded in the concrete and masonry structure, which weighs 130,000 tons.

371
00:19:47,000 --> 00:19:48,220
So it's embedded in that.

372
00:19:48,320 --> 00:19:52,420
At the very bottom, there's a steel plate, and that's what resists the pull.

373
00:19:52,421 --> 00:19:57,240
The cable is pulling on those eye bars, which are then pulling on that plate,

374
00:19:57,360 --> 00:20:01,280
which is trying to lift up that 130,000-pound structure.

375
00:20:01,281 --> 00:20:04,620
and that's why this bridge stays in place.

376
00:20:04,720 --> 00:20:06,620
But Leffert Buck cut corners

377
00:20:06,720 --> 00:20:08,980
with the Williamsburgs cables.

378
00:20:09,080 --> 00:20:12,400
By not using the process of galvanizing the wires,

379
00:20:12,500 --> 00:20:15,460
covering them with a zinc coating to resist corrosion,

380
00:20:15,560 --> 00:20:19,440
he ended up costing New York City a fortune.

381
00:20:19,540 --> 00:20:23,440
They didn't protect that cable and galvanize the wires.

382
00:20:23,540 --> 00:20:27,240
So they put just pure, plain steel wires in that cable.

383
00:20:27,340 --> 00:20:29,640
And of course, within a short amount of time,

384
00:20:29,641 --> 00:20:33,540
of time, water got into the cable and began

385
00:20:33,541 --> 00:20:35,180
to corrode the cable.

386
00:20:35,181 --> 00:20:37,320
Within three years of its opening,

387
00:20:37,321 --> 00:20:39,640
the Williamsburg Bridge needed strengthening

388
00:20:39,641 --> 00:20:40,880
and straightening.

389
00:20:40,881 --> 00:20:45,120
Years later, it was considered so unsafe it was shut down.

390
00:20:45,121 --> 00:20:47,640
If little else, it did what it set out to do,

391
00:20:47,641 --> 00:20:50,460
relieve the congestion on the Brooklyn Bridge.

392
00:20:53,060 --> 00:20:58,460
Next, a Jazz Age addition to the East River.

393
00:20:59,640 --> 00:21:04,640
In 1884, circus owner P.T. Barnum herded 21 elephants

394
00:21:04,641 --> 00:21:08,640
across the Brooklyn Bridge to test its strength.

395
00:21:08,641 --> 00:21:12,640
New York Bridges will return on Modern Marvels.

396
00:21:16,640 --> 00:21:21,640
We now return to New York Bridges on Modern Marvels.

397
00:21:21,641 --> 00:21:24,640
About a half mile upstream on the East River

398
00:21:24,641 --> 00:21:28,640
from the Brooklyn Bridge is the Art Deco Manhattan Bridge.

399
00:21:28,641 --> 00:21:33,080
A second connection between Brooklyn and Manhattan.

400
00:21:33,180 --> 00:21:36,280
Its anchorages rest between Canal Street and Manhattan

401
00:21:36,380 --> 00:21:38,680
and Flatbush Avenue in Brooklyn.

402
00:21:38,780 --> 00:21:42,280
Erected between 1901 and 1909, this

403
00:21:42,380 --> 00:21:45,700
was the third of the East River suspension bridges to be built,

404
00:21:45,800 --> 00:21:49,300
and today is one of the most heavily traveled.

405
00:21:49,400 --> 00:21:54,740
The main span between the towers is 1,470 feet.

406
00:21:54,741 --> 00:21:59,180
Daily, 78,000 vehicles and 350,000 people

407
00:21:59,280 --> 00:22:02,520
use the bridge's seven lanes and two subway tracks.

408
00:22:02,620 --> 00:22:05,080
The bridge's canal approach on the Lower East Side

409
00:22:05,180 --> 00:22:07,180
of Manhattan empties into the arms

410
00:22:07,280 --> 00:22:10,260
of this triumphal, although some consider grandiose,

411
00:22:10,360 --> 00:22:13,120
Beaux-Arts Arch and colonnade designed

412
00:22:13,220 --> 00:22:17,460
by the architectural team of Carrere and Hastings.

413
00:22:17,560 --> 00:22:20,160
The Manhattan end was called the Court of Honor.

414
00:22:20,260 --> 00:22:23,440
And the triumphal arch on the Manhattan end

415
00:22:23,441 --> 00:22:27,640
was modeled on the Port Saint-Denis in Paris

416
00:22:27,740 --> 00:22:29,380
along the Grand Boulevards.

417
00:22:29,480 --> 00:22:31,940
But the bridge that lay just beyond the arts

418
00:22:32,040 --> 00:22:35,420
sorely lacked the grace of its entrance.

419
00:22:35,520 --> 00:22:38,760
When they created the Manhattan Bridge, which in scale

420
00:22:38,860 --> 00:22:41,300
and in usage is completely 20th century,

421
00:22:41,400 --> 00:22:44,720
the Manhattan Bridge was a heavy duty urban bridge

422
00:22:44,820 --> 00:22:49,600
with a vehicle roadway, space for subways and L trains,

423
00:22:49,601 --> 00:22:53,400
trolleys, and of course a pedestrian walkway.

424
00:22:53,500 --> 00:22:56,700
At the Manhattan end and at the Brooklyn end,

425
00:22:56,800 --> 00:22:59,040
they wanted great triumphal arches,

426
00:22:59,140 --> 00:23:01,040
and they hired Carrier and Hastings,

427
00:23:01,140 --> 00:23:03,540
who did the public library on 42nd Street.

428
00:23:03,640 --> 00:23:05,840
If you were to compare, say, the towers,

429
00:23:05,940 --> 00:23:08,760
the stone towers of the Brooklyn Bridge,

430
00:23:08,860 --> 00:23:13,460
which was opened in 1883, to the steel towers

431
00:23:13,560 --> 00:23:16,960
of the Manhattan Bridge, which was opened in 1909,

432
00:23:16,961 --> 00:23:20,460
You'll both notice that they are in a neo-Gothic style.

433
00:23:20,560 --> 00:23:23,540
But one is a much more earthly structure, the Brooklyn,

434
00:23:23,640 --> 00:23:26,540
with all of that handsome stone and the solidity

435
00:23:26,640 --> 00:23:29,540
and the affirmation that that gives.

436
00:23:29,640 --> 00:23:32,580
Whereas by the time 1909 comes about,

437
00:23:32,680 --> 00:23:35,840
you're in the midst of the cast iron age, in a sense.

438
00:23:35,940 --> 00:23:39,120
It is not a cast iron bridge, but its decoration,

439
00:23:39,220 --> 00:23:42,080
the way it's designed, is very reminiscent

440
00:23:42,180 --> 00:23:44,580
of cast iron architecture.

441
00:23:44,581 --> 00:23:47,380
The all steel bridge's ornamental detailing

442
00:23:47,480 --> 00:23:50,260
has long been of special interest to many.

443
00:23:50,360 --> 00:23:52,360
When building the Manhattan Bridge,

444
00:23:52,460 --> 00:23:54,920
once they'd completed stringing the cables,

445
00:23:55,020 --> 00:23:58,400
workers hung the suspender ropes.

446
00:23:58,500 --> 00:24:00,820
These are what comes down and holds the girders,

447
00:24:00,920 --> 00:24:03,560
the stringers in place so that you can actually

448
00:24:03,660 --> 00:24:05,260
start putting down the deck.

449
00:24:05,360 --> 00:24:08,540
And they would usually start around the towers.

450
00:24:08,640 --> 00:24:10,600
Once they had the cables in place,

451
00:24:10,700 --> 00:24:12,240
they had their platforms in place,

452
00:24:12,241 --> 00:24:16,380
in place they would start hanging the suspender ropes and then they would put

453
00:24:16,381 --> 00:24:20,480
the stringers across and they would start next to the towers and then start

454
00:24:20,481 --> 00:24:25,620
building out wood on both sides so then in a sense the bridge grew from the

455
00:24:25,621 --> 00:24:31,260
outside in. It was hard labor it was very long days they didn't have the

456
00:24:31,261 --> 00:24:36,540
ability to have mechanized equipment move heavy blocks and so a lot of it was

457
00:24:36,541 --> 00:24:39,140
is just blood, sweat, and tears.

458
00:24:39,141 --> 00:24:42,300
Leon Moysheef created the two-level Manhattan

459
00:24:42,301 --> 00:24:44,220
suspension bridge.

460
00:24:44,221 --> 00:24:46,500
While it looks light because of its color

461
00:24:46,501 --> 00:24:49,500
and delicate suspension cables, its strength

462
00:24:49,501 --> 00:24:52,260
can be measured in almost 100 years of service.

463
00:24:55,860 --> 00:24:58,620
North of the Williamsburg Bridge,

464
00:24:58,621 --> 00:25:03,700
arching the East River and crossing Roosevelt Island,

465
00:25:03,701 --> 00:25:08,080
is the Queensborough Bridge, situated between 59th

466
00:25:08,180 --> 00:25:11,080
and Manhattan and Long Island City in Queens,

467
00:25:11,180 --> 00:25:15,240
and constructed between 1901 and 1909.

468
00:25:15,340 --> 00:25:17,140
It's the first major bridge in New York

469
00:25:17,240 --> 00:25:20,280
to depart from the cable suspension design,

470
00:25:20,380 --> 00:25:22,520
and a bridge so exciting to the public

471
00:25:22,620 --> 00:25:25,560
with its massive silver painted trusses,

472
00:25:25,660 --> 00:25:30,860
that when it opened, 235 people applied to jump from it,

473
00:25:30,861 --> 00:25:33,560
130 feet into the East River.

474
00:25:33,660 --> 00:25:36,300
The applications were analyzed and broken down

475
00:25:36,400 --> 00:25:39,360
into professional high divers, freaks,

476
00:25:39,460 --> 00:25:42,480
would-be suicides, and the unemployed.

477
00:25:42,580 --> 00:25:45,280
Not one application was approved.

478
00:25:45,380 --> 00:25:48,540
When the Queensborough was open to service in 1909,

479
00:25:48,640 --> 00:25:52,220
you were coming out of the cowboy tradition of the West.

480
00:25:52,320 --> 00:25:55,320
It was very popular for people to go over Niagara Falls

481
00:25:55,420 --> 00:25:58,760
in a wooden barrel, and it would be very popular for people

482
00:25:58,761 --> 00:26:03,460
to leap off the Queensborough Bridge into the river.

483
00:26:03,560 --> 00:26:06,500
Jumping from the center of the $20 million bridge

484
00:26:06,600 --> 00:26:09,640
would have landed the daredevil on Roosevelt Island,

485
00:26:09,740 --> 00:26:12,280
where bridge designer Gustav Lindenthal's

486
00:26:12,380 --> 00:26:15,740
cantilever spans meet.

487
00:26:15,840 --> 00:26:19,080
A suspension bridge is supported from above.

488
00:26:19,180 --> 00:26:21,520
A cantilevered bridge is supported, in this case,

489
00:26:21,620 --> 00:26:23,520
from a tower.

490
00:26:23,620 --> 00:26:26,460
It is supported from the tower, and there is a structure

491
00:26:26,461 --> 00:26:28,860
that allows it to go out in an L shape.

492
00:26:28,960 --> 00:26:32,460
So you have the tower, and then you have a structure above,

493
00:26:32,560 --> 00:26:35,700
a truss-type structure that will allow you that bridge

494
00:26:35,800 --> 00:26:39,200
to stand by itself without anything supporting it above

495
00:26:39,300 --> 00:26:41,280
and without anything supporting it below.

496
00:26:41,380 --> 00:26:44,240
So it's cantilevered.

497
00:26:44,340 --> 00:26:47,380
Two anchor arms extend from opposite shores

498
00:26:47,480 --> 00:26:51,420
and are joined by a middle span, which is supported by piers.

499
00:26:51,520 --> 00:26:55,660
The arms support the midsection by the force of tension.

500
00:26:55,661 --> 00:26:58,260
The piers absorb the downward force.

501
00:26:58,360 --> 00:27:01,200
The trusses are metal triangles that support the floor

502
00:27:01,300 --> 00:27:03,500
either above or below.

503
00:27:03,600 --> 00:27:06,640
It was going to be designed to link Manhattan

504
00:27:06,740 --> 00:27:09,840
with Blackwell's Island, now known as Roosevelt Island,

505
00:27:09,940 --> 00:27:12,040
and Queens.

506
00:27:12,140 --> 00:27:16,620
And essentially, they knew it was going to be for trains.

507
00:27:16,720 --> 00:27:18,920
And they knew that the suspension bridge was not

508
00:27:19,020 --> 00:27:20,980
the best form because of the fact

509
00:27:21,080 --> 00:27:24,320
that if you had trains moving across the span

510
00:27:24,321 --> 00:27:27,860
and they were not in tandem, the bridge would have a tendency

511
00:27:27,960 --> 00:27:29,420
to deflect.

512
00:27:29,520 --> 00:27:33,060
And so the trusses were considered much stronger

513
00:27:33,160 --> 00:27:35,460
for rail traffic.

514
00:27:35,560 --> 00:27:37,760
The scheduled opening of the Queensborough

515
00:27:37,860 --> 00:27:39,860
was delayed when another cantilever

516
00:27:39,960 --> 00:27:43,080
span, the Quebec Bridge, which stretched across the St.

517
00:27:43,180 --> 00:27:46,240
Lawrence River, collapsed.

518
00:27:46,340 --> 00:27:48,320
The public was horrified.

519
00:27:48,420 --> 00:27:50,080
The Scientific American reported

520
00:27:50,180 --> 00:27:52,620
that dangerous changes had been made to designer

521
00:27:52,621 --> 00:27:55,820
Lindenthal's plan and that the Queensborough Bridge

522
00:27:55,920 --> 00:27:57,620
could also fail.

523
00:27:57,720 --> 00:28:00,260
They called in experts to test the bridge's strength

524
00:28:00,360 --> 00:28:03,260
and work continued on the bridge approaches.

525
00:28:03,360 --> 00:28:06,460
The Queensborough Bridge was redesigned and opened

526
00:28:06,560 --> 00:28:10,080
on March 30, 1909.

527
00:28:10,180 --> 00:28:12,780
Despite Lindenthal's best efforts,

528
00:28:12,880 --> 00:28:15,780
the Queensborough Bridge is clumsy looking

529
00:28:15,880 --> 00:28:18,720
and its decorative features are out of place.

530
00:28:18,820 --> 00:28:22,620
Henry F. Hornbostel, the bridge's consulting architect,

531
00:28:22,621 --> 00:28:25,860
reportedly said when he first saw the finished bridge,

532
00:28:25,960 --> 00:28:28,560
my god, it's a blacksmith's shop.

533
00:28:28,660 --> 00:28:33,760
For the citizenry of Queens, the 7,449-foot bridge

534
00:28:33,860 --> 00:28:37,560
was a fiscal windfall.

535
00:28:37,660 --> 00:28:39,040
When they built the Queensborough Bridge,

536
00:28:39,140 --> 00:28:41,900
it was built literally into a rural county.

537
00:28:42,000 --> 00:28:45,580
There's a famous photograph of the Queensborough Bridge

538
00:28:45,680 --> 00:28:48,780
being built, taken from Long Island City,

539
00:28:48,880 --> 00:28:51,280
a rural Long Island City.

540
00:28:51,281 --> 00:28:55,380
and you are literally watching the city and the future coming

541
00:28:55,480 --> 00:28:57,280
to Queens County.

542
00:28:57,380 --> 00:28:59,780
Real estate development spread fast.

543
00:28:59,880 --> 00:29:01,220
Farms were leveled.

544
00:29:01,320 --> 00:29:02,520
Roads were paved.

545
00:29:02,620 --> 00:29:04,920
Trolley lines from the Queens side of the bridge

546
00:29:05,020 --> 00:29:07,620
were laid to previously remote areas.

547
00:29:07,720 --> 00:29:12,640
Queens population of 275,000 ran up to over 1 million residents

548
00:29:12,740 --> 00:29:14,880
by the 1930s.

549
00:29:14,980 --> 00:29:17,780
Today, the bridge formerly known as the Queensborough

550
00:29:17,781 --> 00:29:22,660
is called the 59th Street Bridge by locals, the same 59th Street

551
00:29:22,760 --> 00:29:26,160
Bridge made famous by Simon and Garfunkel.

552
00:29:26,260 --> 00:29:27,660
From the Queensborough Bridge, you

553
00:29:27,760 --> 00:29:30,560
can see impressive views of the Chrysler Building

554
00:29:30,660 --> 00:29:32,600
and the United Nations.

555
00:29:32,700 --> 00:29:35,860
To get that view on foot, prepare to walk on metal grates

556
00:29:35,960 --> 00:29:40,100
that vibrate as the traffic thunders by.

557
00:29:40,200 --> 00:29:44,140
Next, the first span across the mighty Hudson River.

558
00:29:44,141 --> 00:29:49,140
The George Washington Bridge is the most heavily trafficked

559
00:29:49,240 --> 00:29:55,080
of all New York City's bridges, carrying 95.8 million cars

560
00:29:55,180 --> 00:29:56,080
a year.

561
00:29:56,180 --> 00:29:59,280
New York Bridges will continue on Modern Marvels.

562
00:30:03,360 --> 00:30:08,940
We now return to New York Bridges on Modern Marvels.

563
00:30:09,040 --> 00:30:11,540
It was a technological tour de force,

564
00:30:11,541 --> 00:30:15,240
Tour de force, this new bridge that crossed the Hudson River

565
00:30:15,340 --> 00:30:19,240
from northern Manhattan to Fort Lee, New Jersey.

566
00:30:19,340 --> 00:30:24,280
Begun in 1927 and dedicated in October of 1931,

567
00:30:24,380 --> 00:30:28,560
President Franklin D. Roosevelt called the $59 million

568
00:30:28,660 --> 00:30:35,700
George Washington Bridge almost superhuman in perfection.

569
00:30:35,800 --> 00:30:38,500
What Amund did with this bridge that made it so significant

570
00:30:38,600 --> 00:30:41,300
in engineering terms is he applied a new theory

571
00:30:41,301 --> 00:30:43,100
called the deflection theory.

572
00:30:43,200 --> 00:30:45,340
Now, basically, what the deflection theory said

573
00:30:45,440 --> 00:30:48,640
was that when you get a bridge of a certain length,

574
00:30:48,740 --> 00:30:53,240
its size is so big that the bridge itself

575
00:30:53,340 --> 00:30:57,420
can steady itself from the effects of moving traffic

576
00:30:57,520 --> 00:30:58,380
and wind.

577
00:30:58,480 --> 00:31:00,680
Amann reasoned that the stiffening trusses

578
00:31:00,780 --> 00:31:04,120
used to check the flexibility of earlier suspension bridges

579
00:31:04,220 --> 00:31:07,460
like the Manhattan that were designed for railroad traffic

580
00:31:07,560 --> 00:31:10,700
were not necessary on the GWB.

581
00:31:10,701 --> 00:31:12,360
The dead weight of the bridge deck,

582
00:31:12,460 --> 00:31:14,440
along with the four great cables,

583
00:31:14,540 --> 00:31:17,500
would be ample to withstand heavy wind.

584
00:31:17,600 --> 00:31:20,540
After the bridge's steel towers were sunken firmly

585
00:31:20,640 --> 00:31:23,940
into rock and concrete, the rope cables were strung

586
00:31:24,040 --> 00:31:25,640
from the anchorages.

587
00:31:25,740 --> 00:31:28,660
Two spinning wheels, one at the anchorage site in Fort Lee,

588
00:31:28,760 --> 00:31:31,860
New Jersey, the other at the anchorage site in Manhattan,

589
00:31:31,960 --> 00:31:35,200
traveled back and forth, creating strands,

590
00:31:35,300 --> 00:31:39,220
then spun into one of four cables three feet in diameter,

591
00:31:39,221 --> 00:31:42,960
a mile long and strong enough to resist the pull

592
00:31:43,060 --> 00:31:45,460
of 1,000 locomotives.

593
00:31:45,560 --> 00:31:49,500
At completion, the GWB spanned almost twice the distance

594
00:31:49,600 --> 00:31:53,300
of its 19th century East River rival, the Brooklyn Bridge,

595
00:31:53,400 --> 00:31:56,680
making it the longest suspension bridge in the world

596
00:31:56,780 --> 00:31:59,940
and another career achievement for its chief engineer,

597
00:32:00,040 --> 00:32:01,440
Othmar Amman.

598
00:32:01,540 --> 00:32:04,220
It almost doubled the length of any previous bridge

599
00:32:04,320 --> 00:32:05,840
in terms of its span.

600
00:32:05,841 --> 00:32:10,140
It's 3,500 feet long between the towers,

601
00:32:10,240 --> 00:32:12,540
which is the equivalent of 15 city blocks.

602
00:32:12,640 --> 00:32:16,680
It's like Madison Avenue between 42nd Street and 57th

603
00:32:16,780 --> 00:32:20,920
streets, elevated six stories above the ground,

604
00:32:21,020 --> 00:32:24,420
twice as wide, and thronged with buses and cars.

605
00:32:24,520 --> 00:32:27,960
A great admirer of Roebling's Brooklyn Bridge,

606
00:32:28,060 --> 00:32:31,260
Ahman originally planned to jacket the George Washington

607
00:32:31,360 --> 00:32:33,160
in stone.

608
00:32:33,161 --> 00:32:37,240
The 604-foot towers would house an observation deck

609
00:32:37,340 --> 00:32:38,640
in restaurants.

610
00:32:38,740 --> 00:32:41,840
But shortage of money forced him to abandon that idea

611
00:32:41,940 --> 00:32:46,400
in favor of the square-edged, 335-foot silver-colored towers

612
00:32:46,500 --> 00:32:48,140
we see today.

613
00:32:48,240 --> 00:32:51,020
Millions of white-hot rivets driven into place

614
00:32:51,120 --> 00:32:56,280
by pneumatic hammers hold the more than 43,000 tons of steel

615
00:32:56,380 --> 00:32:58,860
together.

616
00:32:58,960 --> 00:33:00,620
In the beginning of the design, he

617
00:33:00,621 --> 00:33:03,660
He felt that some masonry in the form of concrete

618
00:33:03,760 --> 00:33:06,660
would be necessary from an engineering standpoint.

619
00:33:06,760 --> 00:33:09,420
So the idea was just that the steel towers

620
00:33:09,520 --> 00:33:12,160
would be sufficient to hold up the structure

621
00:33:12,260 --> 00:33:14,460
during construction, but that concrete

622
00:33:14,560 --> 00:33:17,900
would have to be poured on the outside layers of the steel

623
00:33:18,000 --> 00:33:20,640
to provide enough strength to actually support the traffic

624
00:33:20,740 --> 00:33:22,300
once the bridge was open.

625
00:33:22,400 --> 00:33:24,040
As they developed in the engineering,

626
00:33:24,140 --> 00:33:28,540
they discovered that even the concrete would not be necessary.

627
00:33:28,541 --> 00:33:33,540
The artistic appeal of the exposed latticework, while accidental,

628
00:33:33,640 --> 00:33:36,940
became the most popular feature of the George Washington Bridge.

629
00:33:39,280 --> 00:33:42,220
After a public contest held by the Port Authority

630
00:33:42,320 --> 00:33:47,000
to choose a name for the bridge, George Washington won.

631
00:33:47,100 --> 00:33:49,300
General Washington's fortifications

632
00:33:49,400 --> 00:33:52,000
occupied the GWB's anchorage sites

633
00:33:52,100 --> 00:33:54,240
when he led the American troops against the British

634
00:33:54,340 --> 00:33:58,540
in the battle for New York in the Revolutionary War.

635
00:33:58,541 --> 00:34:03,540
Next, the bridge that unified the boroughs of New York City.

636
00:34:05,180 --> 00:34:11,180
In 1885, Robert E. Audlum was the first to jump from the Brooklyn Bridge.

637
00:34:11,181 --> 00:34:15,700
He died 45 minutes after he was pulled from the East River.

638
00:34:15,701 --> 00:34:19,700
