640
00:34:27,541 --> 00:34:29,040
levels.

641
00:34:31,220 --> 00:34:34,320
When the bridge that tied together the boroughs of Manhattan,

642
00:34:34,420 --> 00:34:37,720
the Bronx, and Queens over Wards and Randall's Islands

643
00:34:37,820 --> 00:34:42,160
opened to traffic on July 11, 1936,

644
00:34:42,260 --> 00:34:44,760
it was one of the largest public works projects

645
00:34:44,860 --> 00:34:45,820
in the United States.

646
00:34:49,520 --> 00:34:53,600
In a sweltering heat wave, 200,000 citizens

647
00:34:53,700 --> 00:34:56,900
turned out to be the first to cross the Triborough Bridge

648
00:34:56,901 --> 00:34:59,840
That it was ever completed was due in large part

649
00:34:59,940 --> 00:35:04,840
to the complicated and powerful Robert Moses, who in turn

650
00:35:04,940 --> 00:35:07,540
brought in the designer of the George Washington Bridge,

651
00:35:07,640 --> 00:35:09,040
Othmar Amun.

652
00:35:09,140 --> 00:35:12,020
ROBERT MOSES WAS THE GOLDEN BOY OF NEW YORK

653
00:35:12,120 --> 00:35:15,780
OF THE 1920s AND 30s, even 40s.

654
00:35:15,880 --> 00:35:18,560
People of that generation who are still alive,

655
00:35:18,660 --> 00:35:21,600
they think Robert Moses is practically next to God

656
00:35:21,700 --> 00:35:25,720
because they felt he was the only man who could get things done.

657
00:35:25,721 --> 00:35:29,320
Ironically, one day after construction on the bridge

658
00:35:29,420 --> 00:35:33,300
began in 1929, the stock market crashed,

659
00:35:33,400 --> 00:35:35,200
triggering the Great Depression.

660
00:35:35,300 --> 00:35:37,940
And the $5.4 million for its construction

661
00:35:38,040 --> 00:35:40,960
was spent even before its peers were finished.

662
00:35:41,060 --> 00:35:44,040
It wasn't until 1933 that construction resumed

663
00:35:44,140 --> 00:35:46,440
under the supervision of Robert Moses,

664
00:35:46,540 --> 00:35:49,580
the chairman of the new Triborough Bridge Authority.

665
00:35:49,680 --> 00:35:52,320
Through President Franklin Roosevelt's New Deal,

666
00:35:52,420 --> 00:35:55,720
Moses obtained loans and grants of $4 million

667
00:35:55,721 --> 00:36:00,160
$44 million on top of the $5.4 million that had already been

668
00:36:00,260 --> 00:36:01,960
contributed by the city.

669
00:36:02,060 --> 00:36:05,900
Robert Moses, who was a great proponent of automobile

670
00:36:06,000 --> 00:36:11,040
traffic, recognized the need for another bridge

671
00:36:11,140 --> 00:36:16,040
to go from northern Manhattan to Wards Island

672
00:36:16,140 --> 00:36:18,440
and then into Queens.

673
00:36:18,540 --> 00:36:21,240
He wanted to increase access to Long Island,

674
00:36:21,340 --> 00:36:25,720
where he had been installing a number of parks and beaches

675
00:36:25,721 --> 00:36:29,160
and recreation areas of this kind.

676
00:36:29,260 --> 00:36:31,820
Both single-family homes and apartment buildings

677
00:36:31,920 --> 00:36:36,060
were demolished to make way for the bridge approach ramps.

678
00:36:36,160 --> 00:36:38,000
They were given either a month or two notice

679
00:36:38,100 --> 00:36:40,300
to get out of their homes.

680
00:36:40,400 --> 00:36:43,940
And nobody, these were solid working class people

681
00:36:44,040 --> 00:36:45,380
who had invested in their homes.

682
00:36:45,480 --> 00:36:47,240
It was probably the largest investment of their life,

683
00:36:47,340 --> 00:36:50,680
and they were thrown out in a matter of seconds.

684
00:36:50,780 --> 00:36:53,180
Given that Moses built bridges without factoring

685
00:36:53,181 --> 00:36:56,020
Entering in public mass transit and only the middle class

686
00:36:56,120 --> 00:36:59,420
owned cars, people accused Moses of racism

687
00:36:59,520 --> 00:37:02,720
or being just plain anti-poor people.

688
00:37:02,820 --> 00:37:05,860
But Moses had a way of giving to the poor as well as taking

689
00:37:05,960 --> 00:37:07,320
away from them.

690
00:37:07,420 --> 00:37:10,600
So much concrete was needed to pave the Triborough's roadways

691
00:37:10,700 --> 00:37:14,140
that factories reopened and 5,000 unemployed men

692
00:37:14,240 --> 00:37:15,740
went back to work.

693
00:37:15,840 --> 00:37:18,640
High above the bridge, little had changed since the days

694
00:37:18,740 --> 00:37:20,240
of the Brooklyn Bridge.

695
00:37:20,241 --> 00:37:24,080
With the Triborough's main span more than 100 yards high,

696
00:37:24,180 --> 00:37:27,380
painters had to raise themselves up to the top by scaffolds

697
00:37:27,480 --> 00:37:29,980
they built using ropes and pulleys.

698
00:37:30,080 --> 00:37:32,980
If a man didn't fall to his death off the scaffold,

699
00:37:33,080 --> 00:37:37,600
he could still die of lead poisoning from the paint.

700
00:37:37,700 --> 00:37:41,100
Triborough's main span is a half mile long suspension bridge

701
00:37:41,200 --> 00:37:44,500
arching the well-named Hell Gate, the body of water

702
00:37:44,600 --> 00:37:49,100
between Ward's Island and Astoria in Queens.

703
00:37:49,101 --> 00:37:52,200
Within its anchorages are cables containing enough wire

704
00:37:52,300 --> 00:37:55,180
to travel twice around the world.

705
00:37:55,280 --> 00:37:58,280
Robert Caro, author of The Power Broker, a book

706
00:37:58,380 --> 00:38:02,180
about Robert Moses, described the Triborough as not a bridge

707
00:38:02,280 --> 00:38:04,320
so much as a traffic machine.

708
00:38:10,120 --> 00:38:12,920
With all the historical significance collectively

709
00:38:13,020 --> 00:38:15,120
held by the bridges of New York City,

710
00:38:15,220 --> 00:38:18,300
it's a surprise that 56% of the bridges

711
00:38:18,301 --> 00:38:22,480
operated by the city need extensive reconstruction.

712
00:38:22,580 --> 00:38:25,500
Over the years, the East River bridges in particular

713
00:38:25,600 --> 00:38:28,840
were ignored, not by the residents who marveled

714
00:38:28,940 --> 00:38:32,980
at their design, not by commuters dependent upon a land

715
00:38:33,080 --> 00:38:34,980
link to get them from here to there,

716
00:38:35,080 --> 00:38:36,920
but by the city of New York.

717
00:38:37,020 --> 00:38:39,660
In 80 years, these bridges to the past

718
00:38:39,760 --> 00:38:42,560
were barely maintained.

719
00:38:42,660 --> 00:38:44,200
I would say it's a maintenance problem.

720
00:38:44,300 --> 00:38:46,100
I think the bridges were over-designed.

721
00:38:46,101 --> 00:38:47,740
They're very strong.

722
00:38:47,840 --> 00:38:50,680
And if they weren't, we probably would have had a tragedy

723
00:38:50,780 --> 00:38:52,180
in New York already.

724
00:38:52,280 --> 00:38:55,640
The steel is still rusting on the Manhattan Bridge,

725
00:38:55,740 --> 00:38:59,280
and it has torsional or twisting problems.

726
00:38:59,380 --> 00:39:01,480
Its designer put its subway tracks

727
00:39:01,580 --> 00:39:05,160
on the outer edges of the bridge instead of the middle.

728
00:39:05,260 --> 00:39:07,620
When a loaded train comes across the Manhattan,

729
00:39:07,720 --> 00:39:12,060
the bridge twists toward the side on which the train is running.

730
00:39:12,160 --> 00:39:14,660
The bridge has been strengthened after a closing,

731
00:39:14,661 --> 00:39:17,600
But the problem continues.

732
00:39:17,700 --> 00:39:21,560
Sam Schwartz inherited that design flaw on the Manhattan

733
00:39:21,660 --> 00:39:25,100
as well as serious corrosion problems in the Williamsburg

734
00:39:25,200 --> 00:39:27,200
after he was appointed chief engineer

735
00:39:27,300 --> 00:39:29,340
of the Department of Transportation

736
00:39:29,440 --> 00:39:31,140
for the city of New York.

737
00:39:31,240 --> 00:39:35,840
I inherited the bridge, and I had seen all these calculations.

738
00:39:35,940 --> 00:39:40,920
And I called in a number of scientists who,

739
00:39:41,020 --> 00:39:43,260
reading these calculations, said, you have a real problem.

740
00:39:43,261 --> 00:39:43,961
problem.

741
00:39:43,962 --> 00:39:50,780
This bridge may not survive, and this is 1986, 1987, another five years, and there is even a

742
00:39:50,781 --> 00:39:54,577
probability that the bridge may not make it another day.

743
00:39:54,578 --> 00:39:57,560
And so I went over scenarios of what happens

744
00:39:57,561 --> 00:40:01,413
when a suspension bridge fails, and it is not a happy outcome.

745
00:40:01,414 --> 00:40:03,340
It's not as if there's warning.

746
00:40:04,120 --> 00:40:10,000
A suspension bridge had failed just 20 years earlier over the Ohio River, the Silver Bridge

747
00:40:10,001 --> 00:40:11,060
over the Ohio River.

748
00:40:11,160 --> 00:40:13,140
And the description of that failure

749
00:40:13,240 --> 00:40:16,200
was that there was a momentary shake.

750
00:40:16,300 --> 00:40:18,400
And then it fell right to the river

751
00:40:18,500 --> 00:40:21,840
with about 45 people on it who all perished.

752
00:40:21,940 --> 00:40:25,020
As early as 1920, engineers noticed cable wires

753
00:40:25,120 --> 00:40:28,320
beginning to break and corrosion in various wires

754
00:40:28,420 --> 00:40:30,480
on the Williamsburg Bridge.

755
00:40:30,580 --> 00:40:33,420
They applied linseed oil and fish oil to stop it,

756
00:40:33,520 --> 00:40:35,920
and then decided to wrap the wire with zinc.

757
00:40:36,020 --> 00:40:38,820
But the zinc next to the steel resulted

758
00:40:38,821 --> 00:40:41,460
in an electric potential that created more corrosion

759
00:40:41,560 --> 00:40:44,420
instead of alleviating the problem.

760
00:40:44,520 --> 00:40:46,020
This is absolutely a crisis.

761
00:40:46,120 --> 00:40:49,260
And when I first witnessed this in 1988,

762
00:40:49,360 --> 00:40:52,160
I found situations that were even worse than this.

763
00:40:52,260 --> 00:40:55,780
I found a column like this that was split right down the middle.

764
00:40:55,880 --> 00:41:00,340
And there were many others in which were paper-thin steel.

765
00:41:00,440 --> 00:41:04,080
And as a result, we had to close all traffic from the bridge.

766
00:41:04,180 --> 00:41:07,820
We had to stop the trains, stop the cars, the trucks,

767
00:41:07,821 --> 00:41:10,780
and even the pedestrians from walking across the bridge

768
00:41:10,781 --> 00:41:13,220
until we made some temporary repairs.

769
00:41:13,221 --> 00:41:15,620
Many of those temporary repairs are in place,

770
00:41:15,621 --> 00:41:17,660
and now half the bridge has been reconstructed

771
00:41:17,661 --> 00:41:19,740
and the other half will be reconstructed

772
00:41:19,741 --> 00:41:21,660
over the next few years.

773
00:41:21,661 --> 00:41:25,180
The problem here is this cable over here,

774
00:41:25,181 --> 00:41:31,240
these vertical cables, along with the other suspender cables,

775
00:41:31,241 --> 00:41:33,240
they cradle the truss,

776
00:41:33,241 --> 00:41:35,640
so they're attached to each other at the bottom.

777
00:41:35,641 --> 00:41:40,480
That means if one major cable snaps or has a problem,

778
00:41:40,580 --> 00:41:42,680
both cables are useless.

779
00:41:42,780 --> 00:41:45,780
The scariest moment I had was in trying to learn

780
00:41:45,880 --> 00:41:48,680
what is the disaster mode, what happens

781
00:41:48,780 --> 00:41:50,680
if a cable would break.

782
00:41:50,780 --> 00:41:53,460
And if a cable would break, the second cable would break,

783
00:41:53,560 --> 00:41:56,460
that means this side of the bridge would have no support.

784
00:41:56,560 --> 00:41:59,460
Effectively, the roadway would tilt,

785
00:41:59,560 --> 00:42:01,460
and everything on it obviously would plummet

786
00:42:01,560 --> 00:42:02,540
into the East River.

787
00:42:05,640 --> 00:42:09,640
In the redesign for the bridge, that's being corrected.

788
00:42:09,740 --> 00:42:13,180
So these suspender cables will be attached to the truss,

789
00:42:13,280 --> 00:42:15,280
independent of those suspender cables,

790
00:42:15,380 --> 00:42:18,480
which will also be anchored into the truss.

791
00:42:18,580 --> 00:42:21,380
Currently, all the East River bridges share funds

792
00:42:21,480 --> 00:42:25,000
under a multi-billion dollar federal bridge replacement act.

793
00:42:25,100 --> 00:42:26,100
It's not a question of money.

794
00:42:26,200 --> 00:42:29,400
It's how fast can we get the contractor to work.

795
00:42:29,500 --> 00:42:32,840
And my hope is the area of the bridge that we just walked

796
00:42:32,841 --> 00:42:35,980
walked across will be closed down soon,

797
00:42:35,981 --> 00:42:38,440
and traffic will be able to use the other half

798
00:42:38,441 --> 00:42:40,680
of the bridge that's being completed,

799
00:42:40,681 --> 00:42:43,780
and that whole portion will be replaced.

800
00:42:43,781 --> 00:42:44,960
It can't be saved.

801
00:42:44,961 --> 00:42:47,060
When a bridge gets to this point,

802
00:42:47,061 --> 00:42:50,300
and the corrosion is so pervasive,

803
00:42:50,301 --> 00:42:52,860
you cannot save this bridge.

804
00:42:52,861 --> 00:42:56,460
It is unthinkable that I could just take pieces of steel,

805
00:42:56,461 --> 00:43:00,360
I'm not Superman, and I could just tear pieces of steel,

806
00:43:00,361 --> 00:43:03,800
shake them loose, and take them off the bridge.

807
00:43:03,801 --> 00:43:04,800
This can't be saved.

808
00:43:04,801 --> 00:43:06,100
It has to be replaced.

809
00:43:06,101 --> 00:43:08,400
The Williamsburg Bridge, like the others,

810
00:43:08,401 --> 00:43:11,980
never had its expansion joints cleaned or lubricated,

811
00:43:11,981 --> 00:43:14,340
and so they've corroded and frozen.

812
00:43:14,341 --> 00:43:17,540
The structure should expand and contract with the temperature.

813
00:43:17,541 --> 00:43:20,600
Instead, it's pulling itself apart.

814
00:43:20,601 --> 00:43:24,020
Reclamation of these bridges falls to the iron workers.

815
00:43:24,021 --> 00:43:26,460
The worst of it, the hard hats will tell you,

816
00:43:26,461 --> 00:43:28,340
is the fear of lead poisoning.

817
00:43:28,341 --> 00:43:31,540
Long-term exposure to the lead causes kidney disease,

818
00:43:31,640 --> 00:43:35,980
hypertension, stroke, nerve damage, and mental impairment.

819
00:43:36,080 --> 00:43:39,280
The sheer physical demand, the hazards associated

820
00:43:39,380 --> 00:43:44,020
with lead exposure, the prospect of heavy objects swinging

821
00:43:44,120 --> 00:43:47,560
into you or falling onto you under very changeable

822
00:43:47,660 --> 00:43:49,320
and difficult conditions, I think

823
00:43:49,420 --> 00:43:53,240
requires a level of just simple bravery.

824
00:43:53,340 --> 00:43:55,900
Using a pneumatic drill they call the hell dog

825
00:43:56,000 --> 00:43:58,340
to bust old rivets, workers grind

826
00:43:58,341 --> 00:44:00,500
and scrape away at the old paint.

827
00:44:00,501 --> 00:44:02,180
Workers have tracked lead-filled dust

828
00:44:02,181 --> 00:44:05,180
into their own homes, infecting their families.

829
00:44:05,181 --> 00:44:06,780
And what about the communities that

830
00:44:06,781 --> 00:44:08,840
live at the base of these bridges?

831
00:44:08,841 --> 00:44:13,580
Historically, they are the poorest citizens in New York.

832
00:44:13,581 --> 00:44:20,760
No one has yet done the study to look at lead that has really

833
00:44:20,761 --> 00:44:22,700
come from deterioration of bridge structures

834
00:44:22,701 --> 00:44:24,980
to see if that gets into the community

835
00:44:24,981 --> 00:44:27,780
and whether that is causing elevated lead levels

836
00:44:27,781 --> 00:44:30,840
among especially children, because it's children who

837
00:44:30,841 --> 00:44:34,980
play in soil, in dust, and who put things in their mouths

838
00:44:34,981 --> 00:44:37,440
and with contaminated hands and toys

839
00:44:37,441 --> 00:44:40,380
have an opportunity to swallow lead rather than breathe it.

840
00:44:40,381 --> 00:44:45,540
The commonly accepted wisdom that one paint chip the size

841
00:44:45,541 --> 00:44:49,300
of your little fingernail, my little fingernail,

842
00:44:49,301 --> 00:44:53,720
would be enough to render a young child severely toxic.

843
00:44:53,721 --> 00:44:56,100
Fortunately, problems of toxicity

844
00:44:56,101 --> 00:44:59,000
as work continues on the East River spans.

845
00:45:02,740 --> 00:45:05,540
The Williamsburg will have a new subway track.

846
00:45:08,340 --> 00:45:10,440
The Brooklyn, a new deck.

847
00:45:12,680 --> 00:45:14,660
The Manhattan, new paint.

848
00:45:16,660 --> 00:45:20,200
The bridges of New York City are bridges to our past,

849
00:45:20,300 --> 00:45:22,200
as well as our future.

850
00:45:22,300 --> 00:45:25,320
As the 19th century architect Montgomery Schein

851
00:45:25,321 --> 00:45:29,760
Montgomery Shiler said in 1883, the work which is likely to be

852
00:45:29,860 --> 00:45:33,460
our most durable monument and to carry some knowledge of us

853
00:45:33,560 --> 00:45:38,160
to the most remote posterity is a work of bare utility,

854
00:45:38,260 --> 00:45:43,180
not a shrine, not a fortress, not a palace, but a bridge.

